WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.010
Neural style transfer is an incredible technique
where we have two input photographs, and the

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:15.990
output would be a combination of these two,
namely, the content of one and the artistic

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.810
style of the other fused together.

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:23.589
When the first paper appeared on this topic,
the news took the world by storm, and lots

00:00:23.589 --> 00:00:28.699
of speculative discussions emerged as to what
this could be used for and how it would change

00:00:28.699 --> 00:00:31.170
digital arts and the video game industry.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:36.520
It is great fun to use these algorithms and
we have also witnessed a recent proliferation

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:42.090
of phone apps that are able to accomplish
this, which is super cool because of two reasons:

00:00:42.090 --> 00:00:47.850
one, the amount of time to go from a published
research paper to industry-wide application

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:54.280
has never been so small, and, two, the first
work required a powerful computer to accomplish

00:00:54.280 --> 00:01:00.010
this, and took several minutes of strenuous
computation, and now, less than two years

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:04.059
later, it's right in your pocket and can be
done instantly.

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:09.310
Talk about exponential progress in science
and research, absolutely amazing.

00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:15.050
And now, while we feast our eyes upon these
beautiful results, let's talk about the selling

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.640
points of this extension of the original technique.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.770
The paper contains a nice formal explanation
of the weak points of the existing style transfer

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:23.770
algorithms.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:29.050
The intuition behind the explanation is that
the neural networks think in terms of neuron

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:34.320
activations, which may not be proportional
to the color intensities in the source image

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:40.350
styles, therefore their behavior often becomes
inconsistent or different than expected.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:46.180
The authors propose thinking in terms of histograms,
which means that the output image should rely

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:49.470
on statistical similarities with the source
images.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:55.240
And as we can see, the results look outstanding
even when compared to the original method.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:00.430
It is also important to point out that this
proposed technique is also more art directable,

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:03.260
make sure to have a look at the paper for
more details on that.

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.270
As always, I've put a link in the video description.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:11.370
This extension is also capable of texture
synthesis, which means that we give it a small

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:17.630
image patch that shows some sort of repetition,
and it tries to continue it indefinitely in

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:20.530
a way that seems completely seamless.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.650
However, we have to be acutely aware of the
fact that in the computer graphics community,

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:30.590
texture synthesis is considered a subfield
of its own with hundreds of papers, and one

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:35.500
has to be extremely sure to have a clear cut
selling point over the state of the art.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:39.410
For the more interested Fellow Scholars out
there, I've put a survey paper on this in

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:41.620
the video description, make sure to have a
look!

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:46.160
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

